 about this  the official Twitter account for cinema  blends tweeted that the rise of  Skywalker is quote jammed with  fanservice  but the kind that will probably divide  fans and spark heated discussions about  the Star Wars universe  film and television critic Yolanda  Machado sure that she wasn't entirely  surprised by the events of the rise of  Skywalker and felt that quote too much  fanservice was given however those  downsides didn't detract from the  overall greatness of what the rise of  Skywalker does and accomplishes in her  eyes machado tweeted that the film is  quote everything and nothing that you're  expecting she went on to say god I love  this franchise and I'll miss the  Skywalker saga award-winning journalist  and film critic Scott mance had similar  feelings about the fanservice in the  rise of Skywalker there  a lot of it but he really enjoyed the  film as a whole he made no mention of  what the results of packing so much  fanservice into the film might be though  instead keeping his reaction contained  his own excited thoughts he tweeted  really awesome the best of the new  trilogy really fun lots of humor a big  heart so many surprises it's everything  Star Wars fans are hoping for and more a  very satisfying conclusion a lot of  fanservice but I loved it you know the  old saying too much of a good thing is a  bad thing for some critics this adage  applies to the rise of Skywalker  Mashable's Chris Taylor kept his  feelings about the rise of Skywalker's  short and simple unlike what he felt the  movie was he wrote the rise of Skywalker  is a lot it's like 9 movies of plot and  one going to take me about nine days to  process an G jaehun also of Mashable  Eiko Taylor sentiments and tweeting up  the rise of Skywalker is a lot she  expanded quote tons to live here no  shortage of crowd-pleasing moments and  twists and cameos but also quite a bit  to think about han also hinted at it  emotionally fulfilling send-off for  carrie fisher's leia organa